The compositor takes the layer positions of a frame as one set.

Position and bounds origin of a composited layer are written by two threads:
the main thread's layer flush (from the scroll position it last saw) and the
scrolling tree (from the current one), which repositions the scrolled
contents and every fixed and sticky layer after each scroll.  Both wrote the
value the compositor reads, layer by layer, so a frame composited while the
main thread was part way through its flush -- or between that flush and the
scrolling tree applying its positions again -- showed fixed and sticky layers
where the main thread had put them, for the scroll position of a frame ago:
headers that jump by the scroll distance and back on every wheel step.  The
slower the main thread, the more frames fall into that window; on this
system it was most of them while scrolling github.com, chatgpt.com or
redhat.com, which is why patch 0025 used to keep wheel scrolling off the
scrolling thread.

Both writers now leave pending values, the scrolling tree's taking precedence,
and they become current only in CoordinatedSceneState::flushPendingState():
on the main thread at the end of a rendering update, after the scrolling tree
has applied its positions, and on the scrolling thread after it has applied
them on its own.  That commit, for all layers, and the compositor's reading
of positions both hold the scene's state lock, which covers only those
copies: the compositor never waits for a rendering update, and the main
thread never waits for a frame.  The other layer properties need no such
care, since a rendering update cannot start before the frame of the previous
one has been composited.

Same change as WebKit 315356@main (bug 315185, "ThreadedCompositor can
composite a half-committed set of scrolling-tree layer positions"), with its
two follow-ups: 317141@main (bug 319255) guards the layer set, which the
scrolling thread now iterates, with a lock of its own, and 317379@main (bug
319596) takes the state lock only around the position pass of the
compositor's flush.  The deferred tile updates of 315356@main are left out:
they only shortened the time the lock was held over the whole flush, which
317379@main no longer does.

